72	--- Guide for validate-input-after-interaction ---
73	# Validate Input After Interaction
74	
75	## The Problem
76	
77	Displaying validation errors the moment a user focuses on a field and starts typing is premature and distracting. For example, as a user types an email address (e.g., "user@gm") or a password with complex requirements, the field is technically invalid until completion. Standard `:invalid` styling results in an error state appearing immediately, frustrating the user.
78	
79	## The Solution
80	
81	The `:user-invalid` pseudo-class allows you to defer the error state until the user has "committed" to a value (by modifying and blurring the field) or attempted to submit the form. This ensures validation feedback is provided only after the user has finished interacting with the field.
82	
83	### Implementation Strategy
84	
85	1.  **HTML Constraint**: DO use standard HTML5 attributes like `type="email"`, `pattern`, and `required` to trigger the browser's built-in validation logic.
86	2.  **Visual Feedback**: DO use `:user-invalid` to apply error styling only after interaction.
87	3.  **Positive Reinforcement**: DO optionally use `:user-valid` to give a green "success" indicator once the requirements are met.
88	4.  **Graceful Recovery**: As soon as the user corrects the input to a valid format, `:user-invalid` stops matching, removing the error state immediately.
89	5.  **Error Text**: DO show visible error text with the error styling, so the error isn't shown by color alone. DO start each message with an error icon that has a text alternative such as "Error" (the safest choice), or word every message as an error, for example "Please enter a valid email address.", and use the same approach for every error in the form. DO say how to fix the error when you know how, for example "Enter an email address in the format you@example.com."
90	6.  **Error Link**: DO list the error message in the field's `aria-describedby`, after any hint, only while the field is invalid, so screen readers read it with the field. A reference in the markup would read the hidden message from page load. The script in "Consistent ARIA Experience" does this for each field whose `data-error` attribute is set to its error message's `id`.
91	
92	## Implementation Guide
93	
94	### Use Case 1: Email Validation
95	
96	MANDATORY: Rely on standard HTML5 attributes for email fields. The error message is hidden by default and only revealed when the browser determines the user has left the field in an invalid state.
97	
98	```html
99	<form>
100	  <div class="field">
101	    <label for="email">Email Address</label>
102	    <!-- MANDATORY: Place format hints above the input so autocomplete popovers don't cover them during editing -->
103	    <span id="email-hint" class="hint">Format: you@example.com</span>
104	    <!-- DO: Use standard HTML validation attributes like type="email" and required -->
105	    <!-- Set data-error to the error message's id. The aria-invalid script lists that id in aria-describedby while the field is invalid. -->
106	    <input
107	      type="email"
108	      id="email"
109	      name="email"
110	      required
111	      autocomplete="email"
112	      aria-describedby="email-hint"
113	      data-error="email-error"
114	    >
115	    <div id="email-error" class="error-msg">
116	      <span role="img" aria-label="Error">❌</span> Enter an email address in the format you@example.com.
117	    </div>
118	  </div>
119	</form>
120	```
121	
122	```css
123	.hint {
124	  display: block;
125	  color: #5f6368;
126	  font-size: 0.85rem;
127	  margin-bottom: 0.25rem;
128	}
129	
130	.error-msg {
131	  display: none;
132	  color: #d93025;
133	  font-size: 0.875rem;
134	  margin-top: 0.25rem;
135	}
136	
137	/*
138	  DO: Only show error styles after user interaction.
139	  Use multiple indicators (border/background shift + icon/text) to avoid color-only states.
140	*/
141	input:user-invalid {
142	  border-color: #d93025;
143	  background-color: #fce8e6;
144	}
145	
146	/* DO: Reveal the error message using the adjacent sibling selector */
147	input:user-invalid + .error-msg {
148	  display: block;
149	}
150	
151	/* DO: Provide a clear success indication on :user-valid */
152	input:user-valid {
153	  border-color: #188038;
154	}
155	```
156	
157	### Use Case 2: Password Complexity
158	
159	MANDATORY: Define the complexity rule using a Regex Lookahead pattern in the `pattern` attribute. The rules list is shown above the input to guide the user, and highlighted if there's an error.
160	
161	```html
162	<form>
163	  <div class="field">
164	    <label for="password">New Password</label>
165	    <!-- MANDATORY: Place hints and rules above the input so mobile keyboards do not obscure them -->
166	    <ul id="password-rules" class="rules-list">
167	      <li>At least 8 characters</li>
168	      <li>One uppercase letter</li>
169	      <li>One lowercase letter</li>
170	      <li>One number</li>
171	      <li>One special character</li>
172	    </ul>
173	    <!-- DO: Use pattern and minlength for complex password validation
174	         DO: Match all constraints with lookaheads via pattern attribute
175	     -->
176	    <!-- Set data-error to the error message's id. The aria-invalid script lists that id in aria-describedby while the field is invalid. -->
177	    <input
178	      type="password"
179	      id="password"
180	      autocomplete="new-password"
181	      required
182	      pattern="(?=.*\d)(?=.*[a-z])(?=.*[A-Z])(?=.*[\W_]).{8,}"
183	      minlength="8"
184	      aria-describedby="password-rules"
185	      data-error="password-error"
186	    >
187	    <!-- MANDATORY: Give the password its own error text. Turning the rules list red
188	         doesn't say what is wrong, and screen readers don't convey the color change. -->
189	    <div id="password-error" class="error-msg">
190	      <span role="img" aria-label="Error">❌</span> Password doesn't meet all the requirements listed above.
191	    </div>
192	  </div>
193	</form>
194	```
195	
196	```css
197	/* DO: State the default styling as neutral */
198	.rules-list { 
199	  color: #5f6368; 
200	  margin-bottom: 0.5rem;
201	}
202	
203	/* DO: Show invalid state (After interaction): Error */
204	input:user-invalid {
205	  border-color: #d93025;
206	  background-color: #fce8e6;
207	}
208	
209	/* DO: Reveal the error message using the adjacent sibling selector */
210	input:user-invalid + .error-msg {
211	  display: block;
212	}
213	
214	/* DO: Highlight rules list when error is shown using the modern :has() selector */
215	.field:has(input:user-invalid) .rules-list {
216	  color: #d93025;
217	  font-weight: 600;
218	}
219	
220	/* DO: Add success indications for :user-valid state */
221	input:user-valid {
222	  border-color: #188038;
223	}
224	
225	/* DO: Hide rules once satisfied. visibility keeps their space: collapsing the list on
226	   blur would move the submit button between mousedown and mouseup, and the click
227	   would be lost. */
228	.field:has(input:user-valid) .rules-list {
229	  visibility: hidden;
230	}
231	```
232	
233	## Fallbacking & Browser Support
234	
235	### Fallbacks & browser support for :user-valid and :user-invalid
236	
237	Baseline status for :user-valid and :user-invalid: Widely available. It's been Baseline since 2023-11-02.
238	Supported by: Chrome 119 (Oct 2023), Edge 119 (Nov 2023), Firefox 88 (Apr 2021), and Safari 16.5 (May 2023).
239	
240	### CSS for Fallback
241	
242	```css
243	/* Applies to every field that can be invalid (input, select, textarea). :is() keeps the
244	   rule working where :user-invalid isn't supported: in a plain selector list, one
245	   unsupported selector drops the whole rule. */
246	:is(:user-invalid, .user-invalid-fallback) {
247	  border-color: #d93025;
248	  background-color: #fce8e6;
249	}
250	
251	:is(:user-invalid, .user-invalid-fallback) + .error-msg {
252	  display: block;
253	}
254	```
255	
256	### JavaScript Fallback
257	
258	Use a reusable utility that tracks interaction state using a `WeakMap`. This avoids polluting the DOM with "dirty" classes or data attributes.
259	
260	```javascript
261	const UserInvalidFallback = (() => {
262	  const dirtyState = new WeakMap();
263	
264	  const updateState = (input) => {
265	    // validity.valid gives the current state without firing an 'invalid' event, as checkValidity() does.
266	    const isValid = input.validity.valid;
267	
268	    // Update both visual and ARIA state
269	    input.classList.toggle('user-invalid-fallback', !isValid);
270	    input.classList.toggle('user-valid-fallback', isValid);
271	
272	    input.setAttribute('aria-invalid', isValid ? 'false' : 'true');
273	    linkError(input, !isValid);
274	  };
275	
276	  // List the error message (the id in data-error) in aria-describedby only while it's
277	  // shown: text that aria-describedby references is announced even when it's hidden.
278	  const linkError = (input, shown) => {
279	    const errorId = input.dataset.error;
280	    if (!errorId) return;
281	    const ids = (input.getAttribute('aria-describedby') || '').split(/\s+/).filter((id) => id && id !== errorId);
282	    if (shown) ids.push(errorId);
283	    if (ids.length) input.setAttribute('aria-describedby', ids.join(' '));
284	    else input.removeAttribute('aria-describedby');
285	  };
286	
287	  const handleEvent = (event) => {
288	    const input = event.target;
289	
290	    if (event.type === 'reset') {
291	      const controls = input.elements || [];
292	      for (const control of controls) {
293	        dirtyState.delete(control);
294	        control.classList.remove('user-invalid-fallback');
295	        control.classList.remove('user-valid-fallback');
296	        if (control.matches('input, select, textarea')) control.setAttribute('aria-invalid', 'false');
297	        if (control.dataset?.error) linkError(control, false);
298	      }
299	      return;
300	    }
301	
302	    if (!input.checkValidity) return;
303	
304	    // A submit attempt counts as interaction: the browser fires 'invalid' on each failing
305	    // field, including required fields the user never touched, so flag them all.
306	    if (event.type === 'invalid') {
307	      dirtyState.set(input, { hasInteracted: true, hasBlurred: true });
308	      updateState(input);
309	      return;
310	    }
311	
312	    if (event.type === 'input' || event.type === 'change') {
313	      const state = dirtyState.get(input) || { hasInteracted: false, hasBlurred: false };
314	      state.hasInteracted = true;
315	      dirtyState.set(input, state);
316	      if (state.hasBlurred) {
317	        updateState(input);
318	      }
319	    } else if (event.type === 'blur') {
320	      const state = dirtyState.get(input) || { hasInteracted: false, hasBlurred: false };
321	      state.hasBlurred = true;
322	      dirtyState.set(input, state);
323	      if (state.hasInteracted) {
324	        updateState(input);
325	      }
326	    }
327	  };
328	
329	  const init = (root = document) => {
330	    if (CSS.supports('selector(:user-invalid)')) return;
331	
332	    // Firefox and Safari expose an empty required field as invalid from page load.
333	    root.querySelectorAll('input, select, textarea').forEach((el) => {
334	      if (!el.hasAttribute('aria-invalid')) el.setAttribute('aria-invalid', 'false');
335	    });
336	
337	    root.addEventListener('blur', handleEvent, true); // Capture phase
338	    root.addEventListener('input', handleEvent);
339	    root.addEventListener('change', handleEvent);
340	    root.addEventListener('invalid', handleEvent, true); // invalid doesn't bubble
341	    root.addEventListener('reset', handleEvent, true); // Capture resets
342	  };
343	
344	  return { init };
345	})();
346	
347	// Initialize for a specific form
348	const form = document.querySelector('#demo-form');
349	UserInvalidFallback.init(form);
350	```
351	
352	## Other Considerations
353	
354	1.  **Accessibility**:
355	    *   MANDATORY: Use `aria-describedby` to link the rules list to the input.
356	    *   DO NOT: Hide rules lists entirely until the input is valid; users need to know what to type!
357	2.  **Pattern Attribute Limits**: MANDATORY: The `pattern` attribute performs a full match (implied `^...$`). Ensure your password regex accounts for the entire string.
358	3.  **Validation Strictness**: DO note that the browser's default `type="email"` validation is quite permissive (e.g., `user@localserver` might pass). If you need stricter validation, you may need to use a more robust validation library or a custom validation function alongside `type="email"`.
359	4.  **Focus Management**: MANDATORY: If a user submits the form with an invalid field, the browser will automatically focus the first invalid field. Your `:user-invalid` styles will apply immediately because a submission attempt counts as an interaction.
360	5. **Consistent ARIA Experience**: Native `:user-invalid` does not automatically sync with ARIA attributes. Add the following JavaScript to keep `aria-invalid` in sync with the visual state:
361	
362	* Set `aria-invalid="false"` until `:user-invalid` matches, rather than leaving it unset. Firefox and Safari expose an empty `required` field as invalid from page load, and Chromium doesn't expose an empty `required` field as invalid after interaction unless `aria-invalid="true"` is set.
363	* Link each field to its error message by setting the field's `data-error` attribute to the error message's `id` (`data-error="email-error"` for `<div id="email-error">`), and let the script list that `id` in `aria-describedby` only while the field is invalid. Don't reference the error from `aria-describedby` in the markup: the error element is always in the page, and text that `aria-describedby` references is announced even when it's hidden.
364	* Announce an error that appears when the user leaves or changes a field through one polite live region for the page, a moment later so it doesn't talk over the next field. Don't make each error message a live region: a live region that is shown, rather than changed, isn't announced reliably. After a failed submit, move focus to the first invalid field so that its error is announced instead.
365	
366	```javascript
367	// List the field's error message (the id in data-error) in aria-describedby only while
368	// it's shown: text that aria-describedby references is announced even when it's hidden.
369	const linkError = (el, shown) => {
370	  const errorId = el.dataset.error;
371	  if (!errorId) return;
372	  const ids = (el.getAttribute('aria-describedby') || '').split(/\s+/).filter((id) => id && id !== errorId);
373	  if (shown) ids.push(errorId);
374	  if (ids.length) el.setAttribute('aria-describedby', ids.join(' '));
375	  else el.removeAttribute('aria-describedby');
376	};
377	
378	// One polite live region for the page announces an error that appears when the user
379	// leaves or changes a field. Run this once the form is in the page.
380	const announcer = document.createElement('div');
381	announcer.setAttribute('aria-live', 'polite');
382	announcer.classList.add('visually-hidden');
383	document.body.append(announcer);
384	let announceTimer;
385	let announcedField;
386	
387	// Text as a screen reader reads it: icons' text alternatives in, aria-hidden text out.
388	const spokenText = (node) => {
389	  const copy = node.cloneNode(true);
390	  copy.querySelectorAll('[aria-hidden="true"]').forEach((n) => n.remove());
391	  copy.querySelectorAll('[role="img"][aria-label]').forEach((n) => n.replaceWith(`${n.getAttribute('aria-label')}: `));
392	  return copy.textContent.replace(/\s+/g, ' ').trim();
393	};
394	const announceError = (el) => {
395	  const error = document.getElementById(el.dataset.error);
396	  if (!error) return;
397	  const label = el.labels?.[0] ? `${spokenText(el.labels[0])}: ` : '';
398	  clearTimeout(announceTimer);
399	  announcer.textContent = '';
400	  announcedField = el;
401	  // Wait until the screen reader has announced the field that receives focus next.
402	  announceTimer = setTimeout(() => { announcer.textContent = label + spokenText(error); }, 500);
403	};
404	
405	// Sync aria-invalid with the CSS :user-invalid state. Only form fields are synced, and
406	// an aria-invalid="true" that this script didn't set (for example from a server check)
407	// is left alone.
408	const flagged = new WeakSet();
409	// Fields whose current error has been announced, or read out by focus after a failed
410	// submit. A field can become invalid while the user types, so this is tracked separately.
411	const announced = new WeakSet();
412	const syncAria = (el, announce = false) => {
413	  if (!el.matches?.('input, select, textarea')) return;
414	  const invalid = el.matches(':user-invalid');
415	  if (invalid) {
416	    if (announce && !announced.has(el)) {
417	      announceError(el);
418	      announced.add(el);
419	    }
420	    el.setAttribute('aria-invalid', 'true');
421	    linkError(el, true);
422	    flagged.add(el);
423	  } else if (flagged.has(el) || el.getAttribute('aria-invalid') !== 'true') {
424	    el.setAttribute('aria-invalid', 'false');
425	    linkError(el, false);
426	    flagged.delete(el);
427	    announced.delete(el);
428	    // Clear a fixed field's announcement so it isn't found later when reading the page.
429	    if (announcedField === el) {
430	      clearTimeout(announceTimer);
431	      announcer.textContent = '';
432	    }
433	  }
434	};
435	
436	// Start every field at aria-invalid="false".
437	document.querySelectorAll('input, select, textarea').forEach((el) => syncAria(el));
438	
439	// Update on blur (to show and announce an error) and input (to clear it)
440	document.addEventListener('blur', (e) => syncAria(e.target, true), true);
441	document.addEventListener('input', (e) => {
442	  if (e.target.hasAttribute('aria-invalid')) syncAria(e.target);
443	});
444	
445	// Checkboxes, radio buttons and other controls that commit on 'change' can become
446	// :user-invalid without losing focus, so update on change too.
447	document.addEventListener('change', (e) => syncAria(e.target, true), true);
448	
449	// A failed submit fires 'invalid' on each failing field, including required fields the
450	// user never touched, so update those too. 'invalid' doesn't bubble. Focus moving to the
451	// first invalid field announces its error, so these aren't announced.
452	document.addEventListener('invalid', (e) => {
453	  announced.add(e.target);
454	  syncAria(e.target);
455	}, true);
456	
457	// Reset clears :user-invalid after the 'reset' event, so update each field afterwards.
458	document.addEventListener('reset', (e) => {
459	  setTimeout(() => [...e.target.elements].forEach((el) => syncAria(el)));
460	}, true);
461	```
462	
463	Hide the live region with the `.visually-hidden` utility:
464	
465	```css
466	/* Hides content visually but keeps it in the accessibility tree */
467	.visually-hidden:where(:not(:focus-within, :active)) {
468	  position: absolute !important;
469	  clip-path: inset(50%) !important;
470	  overflow: hidden !important;
471	  width: 1px !important;
472	  height: 1px !important;
473	  margin: -1px !important;
474	  padding: 0 !important;
475	  border: 0 !important;
476	  white-space: nowrap !important;
477	}
478	```
